## 1. Introduction
Focusing on the growing energy demand and high dependence on fossil fuels, people are vigorously developing sustainable, highpower and environmentally friendly energy storage and energy conversion devices [1]. Among emerging energy storage systems, supercapacitors have overcome the shortcomings of traditional capacitors and batteries due to their advantages of high charge– discharge energy efficiency, long life, high power density and high safety [2]. It is well known that the specific capacitance (C) and voltage window (V) of a supercapacitor determine its energy density [3,4]. Therefore, to continuously meet people’s highperformance requirements for supercapacitors, it is necessary to develop electrode materials with ideal electrochemical properties and to construct asymmetric and hybrid systems with wide voltage windows [5].
According to the energy storage mechanism, electrode materials include electric double-layer capacitor (EDLC), pseudocapacitive and battery electrode materials [6]. Based on electrostatic adsorption of surface charges, EDLC-type materials are mainly composed of carbon-based materials such as graphene and porous carbon nanosheets, which have received great attention due to their advantages such as large specific surface areas, good stability, electrical conductivity, and high power density [7]. However, carbon materials are widely used as electrode precursors due to their low capacitance values [5,8]. Compared with electric double-layer capacitor (EDLC) materials, both pseudocapacitive and batterytype electrode materials could achieve charge storage through reversible redox reactions, improving specific capacitance and energy density [6], which can be distinguished by their electrochemical curves [9]. Traditional pseudocapacitive materials include typical transition metal oxides/hydroxides/sulfides [10]. However, these traditional pseudocapacitive materials suffer from low electrical conductivity, low rate capacity, and poor electrochemical stability [11]. Therefore, to obtain high-performance electrode materials, it has become widely recognized that the advantages of different materials should be integrated [12].
Besides searching for high-performance materials, scientifically conceiving the structure of electrode materials is also an effective option to improve reaction kinetics. Metal-organic frameworks (MOFs) have attracted extensive attention in the field of supercapacitors due to the advantages of high redox activity, large surface area, regular structure and diverse topological structures [13]. To date, MOF materials have been applied on SC electrodes mainly through two methods: (1) MOF materials are used as precursors and templates to synthesize various derived materials, including carbon/metal oxides [14], carbon/metal hydroxides [15], carbon/ metal sulphides [4] and porous carbon materials [16]. (2) MOFs are directly used as the active components of supercapacitors to store energy directly on the surfaces [17]. The pseudocapacitance of mixed metal centers is higher than those of single metal centers [9]. However, there are usually poor electrical conductivity and chemical stability [18]. In particular, combining MOFs with conductive polymers or conductive carbon materials, such as Ni-MO F/poly(3,4-ethylenedioxythiophene) (PEDOT) [19], $\mathsf{M n}/\mathsf{N i-M O F@~}$ multi-walled carbon nanotubes (MWCNTs) [20], C-Co@MOF [9] and $\mathsf{P A N}/\gamma$ -cyclodextrin-MOF $\upgamma$ -CD-MOF) [21], etc., can not only improve the conductivity and durability of the material but also adjust the morphology of the material, resulting in good electrochemical behavior. However, the poor surface hydrophilicity of carbon-based materials is not conducive to the uniform growth of MOFs [9].
It is worth noting that Tian et al. introduced metal salts into carbon nanofibers (CNFs) by electrospinning technology, which enabled the CNFs to possess high wettability, electrochemical activity, and degree of graphitization [9]. Moreover, CNFs could provide the corresponding metal ions under hydrothermal conditions, enabling the growth of hybrid MOF materials on their surfaces, greatly improving the electrochemical performance. Inspired by the above, biomass-derived, resource-abundant, and low-cost biopolymers, such as chitosan (Cs) [22], alginate [23,24], gelatin [25,26] and cellulose [27], are more environmentally friendly compared to non-degradable synthetic polymers, which has stimulated the enthusiasm of researchers. In particular, carbonized chitosan aerogels (CCA) are formed by interconnecting chitosan molecules to form large-area polymers and forming graphene-like materials during the carbonization process [28]. CCA has the advantages of a high degree of graphitization, large specific surface area, excellent electrical conductivity and selfdoping nitrogen [29]. CCA can be easily hybridized with other components by the sol–gel method and freeze-drying techniques, and be improved in many properties including wettability and electrochemical activity.
In this work, different metals (M, $\mathsf{M}=\mathsf{C o}$ , Ni, Cu, and Zn) were selectively hybridized in chitosan aerogels (CA) by sol–gel method and freeze-drying techniques, and the metals wrapped in carbonized chitosan aerogel. Finally, a solvothermal reaction was performed to use metal nanoparticles for MOF growth. In a typical example, CCA carbon nanosheets decorated with Co nanoparticles (CCA-Co) were covered with MOF microflowers (CCA-Co@MOF) as high-efficiency supercapacitor electrodes. The active centers provided by the Co-doped Ni-MOF (Co-Ni MOF) microflowers with large specific surface areas help improve mass-specific capacitance and durability. Specifically, the obtained CCA-Co@MOF composite exhibits excellent capacitive performance $(1877.2\mathrm{F}\ \mathbf{g}^{-1}$ at $0.5\mathrm{~A~}$ $\begin{array}{r}{{\bf g}^{-1}.}\end{array}$ , excellent rate capability, and good cycling stability ( $85\%$ after 5000 cycles). Furthermore, the asymmetric supercapacitor (CCACo@MOF//AC ASC) assembled using CCA-Co@MOF and activated carbon (AC) electrodes, respectively, exhibits a high energy density (45.9 Wh $\mathbf{kg}^{-1}$ at $431.7~\mathrm{W~kg^{-1}}.$ ), which shows great promise for electrode materials with excellent electrochemical performance.
## 2. Experimental section
## 2.1. Materials
All experimental drugs were analytically pure and could be used without further purification. Chitosan (deacetylation degree $295\%$ , viscosity, $100{-}200~\mathrm{\mPa}{\cdot}s$ ) was obtained from Aladdin. Nickel foams (NF) were bought from Zhengtairong New Material Co., Ltd. Glacial acetic acid (HAc, AR), potassium hydroxide (KOH, AR), hydrochloric acid (HCl, AR), N, $N$ -dimethylformamide (DMF, AR), Terephthalic acid $\mathrm{\cdotH_{2}B D C},$ L, AR), cobalt acetate tetrahydrate $(\mathrm{Co}(\mathsf{A c})_{2}.4\mathrm{H}_{2}0$ , AR), nickel acetate tetrahydrate $(\mathsf{N i}(\mathsf{A c})_{2}.4\mathsf{H}_{2}-$ O, AR), copper acetate monohydrate $(\mathsf{C u}(\mathsf{A c})_{2}.\mathsf{H}_{2}0$ , AR), zinc acetate dehydrate $(Z\mathrm{n}(\mathsf{A}\mathsf{c})_{2}.2\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathsf{O}.$ , AR), activated carbon (AR), acetylene black (AR), polyvinyl alcohol (PVA, AR) and ethanol (AR)were all from Sinopharm Co., Ltd.
2.2. Preparation of CCA-M ${\mathrm{~\boldmath~\cal~M~}}=C O,$ Ni, Zn and $\boldsymbol{c u}$ ), CCA, HAc-CCA and HAc-CCA-Co
2.3. Preparation of CCA-M ${\mathrm{~\cal~M~}}=C o$ , Ni, Zn and Cu)@MOF, CCA@MOF, HAc-CCA@MOF and HAc-CCA-Co@MOF
MOFs were grown on CCA-Co using a solvothermal synthesis method. Typically, $80~\mathrm{mg}$ of CCA-Co was immersed in a solution containing DMF $(50~\mathrm{~ml})$ , ${\mathsf{N i}}({\mathsf{N O}})_{3}{\cdot}6{\mathsf{H}}_{2}0$ $(2.328\ \mathrm{\g})$ and $\mathtt{H}_{2}\mathtt{B D C}$ $(0.236\ \mathrm{g})$ for $^{3\mathrm{~h~}}$ . Afterwards, the mixture was transferred to an autoclave and heated at $120^{\circ}{\mathsf{C}}$ for $16\mathrm{~h~}$ . After washing and drying, the samples were collected and named as CCA-Co@MOF. Similarly, by replacing CCA-Co with CCA, CCA-Ni, CCA-Mn, CCA-Cu, CCA-Zn, HAc-CCA and HAc-CCA-Co, the resulting sample was named as CCA@MOF, CCA-Ni@MOF, CCA-Cu@MOF, CCA-Zn@MOF, HAcCCA@MOF and HAc-CCA-Co@MOF.
## 2.4. Materials characterizations
The appearances on the surface and energy dispersive spectrometer (EDS) spectra of the products were obtained by scanning electron microscope (SEM, Zeiss JSM-F100). Transmission electron microscopy (TEM) and the corresponding selected area electron diffraction (SAED) were all performed on a JEOL JEM-2100 instrument. X-ray diffraction (XRD) was obtained on a Rigaku D-MAXRB diffractometer. The Fourier transform infrared spectra (FTIR) of the samples were obtained by a Bruker spectrometer. Pore size distribution and specific surface area data were determined using an Autosorb-iQ analyzer. X-ray photoelectron spectroscopy (XPS) were obtained using a Thermo Scientific K-Alpha spectrometer.
2.5. Electrochemical measurements.
The electrochemical properties of the prepared samples were tested in a 6 M KOH solution. Generally, the prepared samples as working electrode, $\mathrm{Hg/HgO}$ and $\mathrm{Pt}$ as a reference electrode and counter electrode, respectively. The loading mass of the effective materials was about $2{-}3~\mathrm{mg}$ . The preparation method of electrode in the three-electrode and two-electrode test system has been added in supporting information. Cyclic voltammetry (CV), galvanostatic charge/discharge (GCD) and electrochemical impedance spectrometry (EIS) were measured in the range of $1{-}100\ \mathrm{mV}\ s^{-1}$ , $0.5\substack{-10\mathrm{~A~g~}^{-1}}$ and $10^{-2}–10^{5}~\mathrm{Hz}$ , respectively. The specific capacitance of the sample can be calculated from the GCD curve data and the following equation [9,30,31]:
$$
C_{s p}=\frac{2I\int V d t}{m V^{2}}
$$
Where $\mathsf{C}_{\mathsf{s p}}$ refers to the specific capacitance $\left(\mathsf{F}\:\mathbf{g}^{-1}\right)$ ), $\textstyle\int V d t$ stands for the area of the GCD-discharge curve, I represent the applied current density, $\mathsf{V}$ stands for the operating potential window, m denotes the mass of the active materials.
2.6. Preparation of KOH-based hydrogel electrolyte.
$_{3\mathrm{~g~}}$ PVA was added to deionized water and obtain a homogeneous viscous liquid with heating and stirring. KOH solution (1 M) was added after cooling the solution, and the temperature
was lowered to room temperature to obtain KOH/PVA hydrogel electrolyte [6].
## 2.7. Fabrication and electrochemical tests of asymmetric supercapacitor (ASC)
The ASC device was constructed using the $\mathsf{C C A-C o@M O F}$ cathode and AC anode (CCA-Co@MOF//AC). The charge balance between the CCA-Co@MOF electrode and the AC electrode can be satisfied based on the formula $(q^{+}=q^{-},q=m C\Delta U)$ [8]. The mass loads of CCA-Co@MOF and AC electrodes can be calculated by the following equation [32]:
$$
m^{+}/m^{-}=C^{-}\Delta U^{-}/C^{+}\Delta U^{+}
$$
Where ${\mathsf{C}}^{+}({\mathsf{F}}{\mathsf{g}}^{-1})$ and ${\mathsf{C}}^{-}\left({\mathsf{F}}{\mathsf{g}}^{-1}\right)$ stand for the supercapacitance of CCA- ${\mathrm{.}}{\mathrm{Co@MOF}}$ and AC electrodes, respectively. $\Delta U^{+}$ (V) and $\Delta U^{-}$ (V) represents the voltage range of $\mathsf{C C A-C o@M O F}$ and AC electrodes, respectively. The energy density E (Wh $\mathbf{kg}^{-1}$ ) and power density P $(\mathsf{W\ k g}^{-1})$ ) can be derived from the equations (3), (4) [30,31,33,34]:
$$
E=\frac{I\int V d t}{3.6\times m}
$$
$$
P={\frac{3600\times E}{t}}
$$
Prefabricated KOH/PVA gel electrolytes were used to construct all-solid-state ASC device units. The gel electrolyte was dropped on the surface of the CCA-Co@MOF and AC electrodes to saturation, respectively. The CCA-Co@MOF//AC ASC devices were subsequently construct to obtain all-solid-state ASC devices.
## 3. Results and discussion
## 3.1. Preparation and characterization
Scheme 1 shows the synthetic route of layered CCA-Co@MOF, which mainly consists of three steps. First, CA-Co was prepared by sol–gel method and freeze-drying technique. The synthesized disc-shaped chitosan aerogel (Fig. S1) has a uniform threedimensional connected channel porous structure. Second, the aerogel was carbonized in Ar. Interestingly, after carbonization, the channel walls in the aerogel were destroyed and transformed into cobalt nanoparticle-embedded porous carbon nanosheets aligned in a certain direction. Finally, MOFs were grown on the surface of CCA-Co nanosheets by a typical solvothermal reaction process. During the solvothermal process, the cobalt nanocrystals are etched with $\boldsymbol{\mathrm{H}}^{+}$ provided by $\mathsf{H}_{2}\mathsf{B D C}$ , releasing a portion of the cobalt ions [9]. Meanwhile, the cobalt and nickel ions coordinate with $\mathsf{H}_{2}\mathsf{B D C}$ to prepare Co-Ni MOF microflowers on the CCA-Co surface [35].
The surface morphologies of all samples were investigated with SEM. From the SEM images of HAc-CCA (Fig. 1a and Fig. $S2(\mathbf{a},\mathbf{d});$ , it can be found that the surface and cross-section of the carbon nanosheets obtained by dissolving chitosan with acetic acid are smooth and flat, without porous structure. However, SEM images of CCA (Fig. 1d and Fig. S2(b, e)) shows that the surface of the carbon nanosheets obtained by dissolving chitosan with dilute hydrochloric acid has wrinkles. Importantly, the uniform porous structure is evident from the cross-section of the carbon nanosheets. Porous CCA as support for MOFs growth may be more favorable for electrolyte diffusion and electron transport. With the incorporation of cobalt into the aerogel, cobalt nanoparticles are formed on the surface of HAc-CCA-Co nanosheets after carbonization (Fig. 1b and Fig. S2c). At the same time, many nanoparticles aggregated into cobalt metal microspheres under the action of high temperatures. In addition, Fig. 2c shows that the surface and crosssection of the HAc-CCA-Co nanosheets are relatively dense and have no porous structure. Different from the HAc-CCA-Co sample, the surface of CCA-Co nanosheets (Fig. 1e and Fig. S2f) obtained by dissolving chitosan with dilute hydrochloric acid not only has cobalt metal nanoparticles and cobalt metal microspheres but also has uniformly distributed pores. Furthermore, a CCA-like porous structure can be seen on the cross-section of the nanosheets (Fig. 1f), showing that the incorporation of cobalt does not damage the inner porous structure of the nanosheets. Meanwhile, metal particles and porous structures also exist on the surfaces of CCANi (Fig. S3(a-c)) and CCA-Cu (Fig. S3(g-i)). Notably, the surface of CCA-Zn nanosheets (Fig. S3(d-f)) formed a wrinkled morphology similar to that of the CCA surface. Meanwhile, zinc metal nanoparticles do not exist on the surface of CCA-Zn, which may be due to the volatilization of metal zinc under high-temperature carbonization [9].
From the SEM images (Fig. 2(g-i)) of $\mathsf{C C A-C o@M O F}$ , the MOF microflowers grow uniformly on the surface of the CCA-Co nanosheets. Microflowers with a diameter of about $3~{\upmu\mathrm{m}}$ formed by three-dimensional stacking of ultrathin MOF nanosheets can provide a larger specific surface area, thereby exposing more active sites. Compared with ${\mathsf{C C A@M O F}}$ (Fig. S4(a-c)), the surface of CCA${\mathsf{C o@M O F}}$ is covered with thicker and denser microflowers, indicating that the modification of Co nanoparticles enhances the adhesion between the substrate and MOF. Fig. S4(d-f) are the SEM images of HAc-CCA-Co@MOF, it can be found that the MOF nanosheets are closely stacked on the surface of HAc-CCA-Co, and there are almost no voids between the MOF nanosheets, which will lead to active sites significantly decrease. From the SEM images of CCA-Ni@MOF (Fig. S5(a-c)), Ni MOF nanosheets are uniformly distributed on the surface of CCA-Ni, and there are uniform pores between Ni MOF nanosheets, which can provide a larger ratio of surface area, resulting in more reaction sites. The SEM images of $\mathsf{C C A-C u@M O F}$ (Fig. S5(g-i)) show that Cu-Ni MOF microflowers are sparsely distributed on the surface of CCA-Cu nanosheets. Particularly, Fig. S5(d–f) shows the SEM images of CCA-Zn@MOF, which may be due to the volatilization of zinc metal under high-temperature carbonization, resulting in only Ni MOF microflowers growing sparsely on the surface of CCA. In addition, the absence of zinc element was found from the elemental mapping images (Fig. S6e) at the same location of the CCA-Zn@MOF. Meanwhile, C, N, O, Ni and Co elements were found in the elemental mapping images of the same position of CCA-Co@MOF (Fig. 1j) and HAc-CCA-Co@MOF (Fig. S6c) samples, respectively, indicating that Ni-Co MOF nanostructures were generated by the simple solvothermal reaction. The elemental mapping images of CCA@- MOF (Fig. S6a), HAc-CCA@MOF (Fig. S6b), and CCA-Ni@MOF (Fig. S6d) all contain C, N, O and Ni elements, but do not contain Co element, indicating that Ni MOFs were successfully prepared on the surface of the samples. The elemental mapping images of the CCA-Cu@MOF sample show that it contains C, N, O, Ni and Cu elements, showing that the Ni-Cu MOF was synthesized on the surface of the CCA-Cu.
To further characterize the morphology of the Co-Ni MOFs, TEM tests were performed. Fig. 2a shows the Co-Ni MOF morphology of microflowers, and the nanosheets (Fig. 2b) are found to be nearly transparent by local magnification at the edges of the microflowers, indicating an ultra-flake-like structure. Furthermore, wrinkled and ultrathin Co-Ni MOF nanosheets can provide large active surfaces for charge storage [35]. The high-resolution TEM image (Fig. 2c) reveals that the surface of the nanosheets exhibits uniformly distributed nanocrystallites with a particle size of $3-$ $5\ \mathrm{nm}$ . The evenly dispersed locations marked with red circles are some identical lattice fringes with a lattice spacing of $0.28\mathrm{nm}$ Furthermore, Fig. 2d shows some diffraction spots and rings of confusion, indicating that the synthesized Co-Ni MOF has good crystalline properties. Specifically, lattice fringes of $0.28~\mathrm{\nm}$ (Fig. 2c) can be associated with the (220) crystal plane of Co-Ni MOF (Fig. 2d).
In Fig. $3(\mathsf{a},\mathsf{b})$ , after the carbonization process, ${\mathsf{C o}}(\mathsf{A c})_{2}{\cdot}4\mathsf{H}_{2}0$ , Ni $(\mathsf{A c})_{2}.4\mathsf{H}_{2}0$ and $\mathrm{Cu}(\mathsf{A c})_{2}{\cdot}\mathrm{H}_{2}0$ in CA were converted to Co, Ni and Cu, respectively. ${\mathsf{C o}}(\mathsf{A c})_{2}{\cdot}4\mathsf{H}_{2}0$ in HAc-CA was converted to Co. In particular, the release of zinc element at high temperature resulted in only carbon peaks in the XRD pattern of CCA-Zn, which also fully proves the absence of Zn metal spheres on the surface of CCA-Zn (Fig. S3(d–f)), and the reason for the absence of Zn element in the CCA-Zn@MOF mapping (Fig. S6e). In addition, the products of MOF grown on CCA, HAc-CCA, CCA-Co, HAc-CCA-Co, CCA-Ni, CCA-Cu and CCA-Zn respectively, all showed semblable diffraction peaks (Fig. 3(a, c)), which is consistent with the diffraction peaks of Ni MOFs reported previously [36]. In addition, the FTIR spectra of CCA- ${\mathrm{.}}{\mathrm{Co@MOF}}$ are also obtained to study the coordination states of Co-Ni MOF. In Fig. 3c, the strong bands located at 1583.88 and $1367.62~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ are attributed to the asymmetric and symmetric stretching modes of the –COO group, respectively [37]. Their difference suggests the existence of Ni or Co ions coordinating with ligands through bidentate modes [9]. The band at $3422.99~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ is attributed to the typical stretching vibrations of water molecules, showing the coordinated $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ molecules in the structure [38]. The energy band at $3524.46~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ is the stretching vibration of typical intramolecular hydrogen bonds, which indicates that water molecules are generated in the material structure [9]. The stretching vibrations of the $C{\mathrm{-}}\mathrm{H}$ and OH– groups are assigned to the energy bands of 1504.05 and $3592.47~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ , respectively [36]. Bands below $1000~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ are associated with O-M (O-Ni or O-Co) vibrations.
To further support the results of XRD and FTIR, The XPS analysis of CCA-Co, CCA@MOF and CCA-Co@MOF composites are illustrated in Fig. 3e. The full spectrum of all tested materials indicated the presence of C 1s, O 1s and N 1s. The presence of N1s further indicates that N has successfully self-dopped in the samples. Furthermore, the peak of Co 2p appears in the spectrum of CCA-Co material, which further proves the existence of Co element. However, the Co 2p spectrum (Fig. S12a) of CCA-Co has a non-zero valence state which should be due to the oxidation of a small amount of cobalt metal on the surface of the material [39], causing the appearance of the peaks at 780.37, 795.6 eV for $C0^{2+}$ and 778.4, $794.3\mathrm{eV}$ for ${\mathsf{C o}}^{3+}$ . In addition, Fig. S12b and Fig. S12c show the XPS spectra of Ni 2p and $\mathtt{C u2p}$ for CCA-Ni and CCA-Cu, respectively. In Fig. 3g, the Co 2p of CCA-Co@MOF has peaks at 780.66 $(\mathsf{C o}^{2+})$ and 796.75 eV $(\mathsf{C o}^{3+})$ , respectively, and the lower binding energy of $C o^{2+}$ indicates that it is easier to coordinate with the ligand. The peak of $\mathsf{N i~}2\mathsf{p}$ appeared in the spectrum of ${\mathsf{C C A@M O F}}$ material. Peaks of $\mathtt{C o2p}$ and $\mathsf{N i2p}$ appear in the spectra of $\mathsf{C C A-C o@M O F}$ composites. The C 1s spectrum of CCA-Co@MOF (Fig. S7a) can be divided into the peaks of $\mathsf{s p}^{2}$ , $\mathsf{s p}^{3}$ , CAN, CAO, and $\scriptstyle{\mathsf{C}}=0$ , respectively. The N 1s spectrum (Fig. S7b) of CCA-Co@MOF can be split into peaks of pyridinium nitrogen, pyridine nitrogen and pyridine
N-oxide, respectively. The spectra of $\mathsf{N i2p}$ (Fig. 3f) in CCA@MOF and CCA-Co@MOF show that the peaks of $855.5\ \mathrm{eV}$ and 873.1 eV belong to Ni $2\mathsf{p}_{3/2}$ and Ni $2{\mathsf{p}}_{1/2}$ , indicating that the nickel ions belong to the divalent oxidation state [34]. Fig. 3g shows that the Co content in CCA $-C{\boldsymbol{0}}@\mathsf{M O F}$ is low, and the four fitted peaks of the Co 2p spectrum mainly include the characteristic peaks of Co $2{\tt p}_{1/2}$ and Co $2\mathsf{p}_{3/2}$ , and two satellite peaks. The energy difference of the main peak is $16.1\ \mathrm{eV}$ , which reveals the presence of ${\mathsf{C o}}^{2+}$ [34]. In Fig. 3h, both the O 1s spectrum of CCA-Co@MOF and ${\mathsf{C C A@}}.$ - MOF can be split into peaks of –OH and absorbed water. Possibly due to the introduction of Co ions into the Ni MOF, the position of the O 1s peak of $\mathsf{C C A-C o@M O F}$ shifts to the lower binding energy. These results further reveal that cobalt ions are more inclined to combine with ligands to form bimetallic MOFs and reflect the co-doping-induced changes in surface electronic states [9].
In addition, nitrogen adsorption–desorption analysis was used to measure the surface area (Fig. 3i) and pore distribution (inset in Fig. 3i) of the $\mathsf{C C A-C o@M O F}$ . The CCA- ${\mathrm{.}}C0@\mathbf{M}0{\mathrm{F}}$ with a larger specific surface area $(55.16\thinspace\mathrm{cm}^{2}\thinspace\mathrm{g}^{-1}.$ ) can provide more active sites for charge storage. The pores of CCA-Co@MOF are mainly distributed at $2{-}40~\mathrm{{nm}}$ , which can greatly promote electrolyte diffusion and electron transport.
## 3.2. Electrochemical properties of the prepared electrodes
The electrochemical properties of CCA@MOF, HAc-CCA@MOF, CCA-Co@MOF, HAc-CCA-Co@MOF, CCA-Ni@MOF, CCA-Zn@MOF and CCA- $-{\mathsf{C u@M O F}}$ electrodes were characterized in a threeelectrode system with voltage windows of $_{0-0.5\mathrm{~V~}}$ in a 6 M KOH electrolyte solution. Fig. 4a shows the CVs of the electrodes at $20\ \mathrm{mV}\ s^{-1}$ . Compared with other electrodes, the prepared CCA${\mathsf{C o@M O F}}$ electrode has a larger integrated area of the CV curve and stronger chemical reaction peaks, showing its fast Faradaic reaction kinetics and excellent capacitive performance. In addition, all fabricated electrodes were also tested to obtain GCD curves (Fig. 4b). Furthermore, the discharge time of the CCA-Co@MOF electrode was significantly longer than other electrodes at $0.5\mathrm{~A~}$ $\mathbf{g}^{-1}$ , which was indistinguishable from the CV results. Furthermore, the performance of electrochemical supercapacitors is illustrated based on reactions below [9]:
$$
N i_{3}(O H)_{2}(H_{2}O)_{2}(L)_{2}+O H^{-}\leftrightarrow N i_{3}(O H)_{3}H_{2}O(L)_{2}+H_{2}O+e^{-}
$$
$$
\begin{array}{r l}{N i_{3-x}C o_{x}(O H)_{2}(H_{2}O)_{2}(L)_{2}+O H^{-}\leftrightarrow N i_{3-x}C o_{x}(O H)_{3}H_{2}O(L)_{2}+H_{2}O}&{}\\ {+e^{-}}&{}\end{array}
$$
To deeply evaluate the electrochemical performance of the CCA${\mathsf{C o@M O F}}$ electrode, the CV curves at multiple scan rates and the GCD curves at various current densities were tested. Fig. 4c shows the CV curves of CCA-Co@MOF electrodes at disparate scan speeds from 1 to $100\ \mathrm{mV}\ s^{-1}$ . At low sweep speed, the shape of the CV curve exhibits distinct redox peaks, indicating that most of the capacitance originates from the Faraday reaction. Both the peak current and the area increase with the expansion of the rate, indicating that the material possesses excellent chemical reversibility [1]. Furthermore, in Fig. 4d, the shape of the GCD curve of the CCA-Co@MOF electrode is almost symmetrical at disparate current densities, showing that the as-prepared electrode has a high coulombic efficiency [5]. Meanwhile, the CV (Fig. S8) and GCD (Fig. S9) curves of other prepared electrodes all show similar shapes and trends. The discharge time of the electrodes gradually increased with the decrease of current density, in line with the results of the CV curves. Based on previous reports and the shape of the cyclic voltammetry curve, this MOF material can be classified as a battery type electrode material [34,36]. In addition, the specific capacitance of the electrodes is obtained based on the GCD curve of the electrodes and formula (1). The specific capacitances (Fig. 4e) of the CCA-Co@MOF electrodes at 0.5, 1, 2, 4, 6, 8 and $10\mathrm{~A~g^{-1}}$ are 1877.2, 1817.2, 1645.5, 1500.5, 1392.7, 1300.4 and $1216.6\mathrm{F}\mathrm{\bfg}^{-1}$ , respectively. It can be found that the mass capacitance of the CCA- ${\cal C}_{\mathrm{{0}}}@\mathrm{MOF}$ $\bf{1877.2F~g^{-1}},$ is obviously better those that of CCA@MOF $\zeta1206.6\mathrm{F}\:\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ ), HAc-CCA@MOF $(897.8\mathrm{F}\mathrm{g}^{-1}\cdot$ , HAcCCA-Co@MOF $(1417.6\mathrm{F}\ \mathbf{g}^{-1},$ ), CCA-Ni@MOF $\left(1229.6\mathrm{F}\ \mathbf{g}^{-1}\right),$ , CCA$Z{\mathrm{n@MOF}}$ $\begin{array}{r}{1081.7\mathrm{F}\ \mathbf{g}^{-1},}\end{array}$ and CC $\mathsf{T A}\mathrm{-}\mathsf{C u@M O F}$ $(926.9\mathrm{F}\ \mathbf{g}^{-1},$ ) electrode materials at $0.5\mathrm{~A~g^{-1}}$ . Meanwhile, the specific capacitance of the CCA-Co@MOF electrode in Fig. 4h is similar to or greater than most recently reported electrodes fabricated from related materials [9,34,36,40–47].
Fig. 4f shows the Nyquist plots of all fabricated electrodes derived from frequencies range. Impedance spectroscopy shows that the slope of the CCA-Co@MOF electrode in the lowfrequency region is significantly larger than that of other electrodes, indicating lower diffusion resistance during the Faradaic reaction [10]. In the high-frequency region (inset of Fig. 4f), all the prepared electrodes have no semicircular region, indicating high charge transfer efficiency at the reaction interface [3]. The measured equivalent series resistance (ESR) of the CCA-Co@MOF electrode at the x-axis intercept is similar to other electrodes, indicating that the electrode prepared with carbon material as the substrate has better conductivity. As shown in Fig. $4\mathrm{g},$ the cycling performance of all electrodes was characterized based on GCD experiments. The CCA-Co@MOF electrode retained $85\%$ of its initial capacitance after 5000 cycles, which is better than ${\mathsf{C C A@M O F}}$ $(80\ \%)$ , HAc-CCA@MOF $(65\ \%)$ , HAc-CCA-Co@MOF $(75\ \%)$ , CCANi@MOF $(72\%)$ , CCA-Ni $@$ MOF $(54\%)$ and CCA-Ni@MOF $(46\%)$ electrode materials, indicating that the CCA-Co@MOF electrode has a longer service life and better durability. As shown in Fig. 4i, the CCA- ${\cal C}_{\mathrm{{0}}}@\mathrm{MOF}$ electrode exhibits excellent mass capacitance, rate and cycling capability, which are caused by various reasons. First, as a binder-free current collector, CCA-Co with excellent hydrophilic properties has three significant roles: (1) To provide a substrate for MOF growth, promoting the growth of Ni-Co MOF microflowers. (2) CCA-Co can provide Co during MOF growth. (3) The porous CCA-Co substrate facilitates the entry of electrolytes for store electrons and transport. Second, Ni-Co MOF microflowers provide more reaction sites and electron transfer paths, which can improve the rate capability of CCA-Co@MOF. Furthermore, the Ni-Co MOF microflowers on CCA-Co contain a mixture of Ni and Co, which enables the supercapacitors to possess excellent capacitive performance through sufficient redox reactions.
## 3.3. Electrochemical performance of the CCA-Co@MOF//AC asymmetric supercapacitor device
To further verify the electrochemical performance of CCACo@MOF electrodes in practice, an CCA-Co@MOF//AC ASC was successfully assembled using CCA-Co@MOF and AC electrode. The operating voltage window of the ASC will be co-generated by the AC and CCA-Co@MOF electrodes. Fig. 5a shows the CV curves of the positive and negative electrodes at a scan speed of $20\mathrm{mV}s^{-1}$ . Fig. S10 shows the CV and GCD curves of the AC electrode. The CV curves tested in different voltage ranges $(0.6\mathrm{-}1.8~\mathrm{V})$ at $20\mathrm{mV}s^{-1}$ are shown in Fig. 5b. It can be found that when the voltage is increased to $1.6\:\mathrm{V}$ , the CV curve has no polarization phenomenon caused by excessive voltage, and the shape of the CV curve remains unchanged in various voltage windows. However, when the voltage increased to $1.7\mathrm{V},$ , the curve appeared slightly polarized, and when the voltage rises to $1.8\mathrm{V}$ , the curve showed obvious polarization. Similar results were obtained from the GCD curves (Fig. 5c) measured in different voltage ranges $(0.6\mathrm{-}1.75\mathrm{~V)}$ at $1\mathrm{~A~g^{-1}}$ . The GCD curves are symmetric when the voltage peak increases to
$1.6~\mathrm{V}.$ . The charge curve does not appear in a plateau region in the high voltage region, and the shape remains unchanged in different potential windows, indicating that this ASC device has significant capacitive performance. But when the voltage peaks up to $1.7\mathrm{V}.$ , a slight plateau appears in the charging curve. Seriously, when the voltage peaks up to $1.75{\mathrm{~V~}}$ , the charging curve shows a clear plateau region, showing poor coulombic efficiency [48].
The high operating voltage can condense the number of equipment required to achieve a satisfactory output voltage, which is of great significance in practical applications [3]. To be conservative, we adopted a voltage range of $_{0-1.6\mathrm{V}}$ as the working voltage window of CCA-Co@MOF//AC ASC for subsequent electrochemical performance tests (Fig. 5d-i). At the same time, it was supplemented with subsequent electrochemical performance tests using a voltage window of $0{-}1.7\ \mathrm{~V~}$ as the operating voltage window (Fig. S11). Fig. 5d shows the CV curves of CCA-Co@MOF//AC ASC devices at disparate scan rates of ${5{-}100\mathrm{mV}s^{-1}}$ in a potential range of $_{0-1.6\mathrm{\DeltaV}}$ . Fig. 5e shows the GCD curves of CCA-Co@MOF//AC ASC devices at disparate current densities of $0.5\substack{-10\mathsf{A}\mathsf{g}^{-1}}$ in a potential window of 0–1.6 V. The nearly symmetric GCD curves demonstrate the high Coulombic efficiency and electrochemical reversibility of CCA-Co@MOF//AC ASCs [49]. Fig. S11a and Fig. S11b show the CV and GCD curves of ASC when the voltage range is $0{-}1.7\:\mathrm{V}.$ In addition, the specific capacitances of CCA-Co@MOF//AC ASC devices were calculated by GCD curve and formula (1). Fig. 5f show that the specific capacitances at current densities of 0.5, 1, 2, 4, 6, 8 and $10\mathrm{~A~g^{-1}}$ were 129, 115.8, 101.4, 86.8, 75.5, 67 and 59.4F $\mathbf{g}^{-1}$ , respectively. Meanwhile, the specific capacitance (Fig. S11c) of CCA-Co@MOF//AC ASC is $123.2\mathrm{F}~\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ at 1 A $\mathbf{g}^{-1}$ in the $_{0-1.7\mathrm{~V~}}$ voltage window. Furthermore, Fig. 5g shows that the as-prepared device unit achieves an energy density of 45.9 Wh $\mathbf{kg}^{-1}$ at 431.7 W $\mathbf{kg}^{-1}$ , which is comparable to or higher than those reported in the literatures [9,36,41,45,46]. In addition, the CCACo@MOF//AC ASC (Fig. S11d) achieves an energy density of 41.2 Wh $\mathbf{kg}^{-1}$ at 699.2 W $\mathbf{kg}^{-1}$ in the $_{0-1.7\mathrm{~V~}}$ voltage window. Fig. 5h shows the cycling ability and storage capacity efficiency of the ASC at $2\mathsf{A}\mathsf{g}^{-1}$ . The insets are the CV $(20\:\mathrm{mV}\:s^{-1}).$ curve comparison and the GCD $\cdot1\mathrm{~A~g~}^{-1}.$ curve comparison at the first and 5000th cycle, respectively. The results show that after 5000 cycles, the specific capacitance of the ASC device can maintain $91\%$ of the original capacitance, and the Coulombic efficiency can maintain
$96\%$ indicating that the ASC device has excellent cycling stability and reversibility [8]. Furthermore, to evaluate the practical value of ASCs devices as high-performance supercapacitors or energy storage systems, connecting three prepared all-solid-state ASCs in series can make a small light bulb continue to emit light for $4~\mathrm{min}$ (Fig. 5i).
## 4. Conclusion
In summary, we conceived a simple strategy to grow MOFs on cobalt nanoparticles-modified carbonized chitosan aerogels to obtain CCA-Co@MOF. Compared with other organic synthetic materials, biomass chitosan as carbon source is more environmentally friendly and meets the requirements of sustainable development [22,28,29,50]. Meanwhile, the presence of Co nanoparticles in carbonized chitosan aerogel (CCA) not only promotes the selfassembly of CCA and MOF, etched Co ions can also participate in the growth of MOF, resulting in excellent electrochemical performance. As results, CCA-Co@MOF shows a much high mass capacitance $(1877.2\mathrm{F}\ \mathrm{g}^{-1}$ at $0.5\mathrm{~A~g}^{-1}$ ) compared with CCA-M@MOF $\mathbf{\dot{M}=N i}$ , Zn and Mn), CCA@MOF, HAc-CCA@MOF and HAc-CCA${\mathsf{C o@M O F}}$ . In addition, the obtained CCA-Co@MOF composite exhibits excellent rate capability and good cycling stability ( $85\%$ after 5000 cycles). Furthermore, the CCA-Co@MOF//AC ASC assembled using CCA-Co@MOF and AC electrodes as cathode and anode, respectively, exhibits excellent capacitive performance (129F $\mathbf{g}^{-1}$ at 0.5 A $\mathbf{g}^{-1}$ ) and high energy density (45.9 Wh $\displaystyle{\log^{-1}}$ at $431.7~\mathrm{W~kg^{-1}};$ . This work demonstrates the clever application of metal nanoparticles-modified carbonized chitosan aerogels, and provides an excellent way to prepare bimetallic metal ion MOFbased materials with broad application prospects.
